  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock id="HHC9E9219375AC2F82A8CDFAF00D88FECC" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="text-indent: -24px; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="text-indent: -24px; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Arial, sans-serif;"&gt;All&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;&amp;#160;of the membership interest in Kinetica Partners LLC were purchase by Eckard Midstream LLC, in a transaction that closed on December 27, 2023.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;a style="color: #000000; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;" name="_Hlk193704228"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;The Chairman of the Board of Eckard Midstream LLC owned or controlled a minority membership interest in Kinetica Partners prior to his December 27&lt;/span&gt;&lt;sup style="font-family: sans-serif; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium; text-indent: -24px;"&gt;&amp;#160;purchase of the remaining members&amp;#8217; interests.&amp;#160; There were no changes to the ownership or control of Kinetica Energy Express, LLC.&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantContingentAssetsOrLiabilitiesTextBlock id="HHBF53398AA5137162F0C0A6B245F0BF61" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantContingentAssetsOrLiabilitiesTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfPensionPostRetirementBenefitOtherThanPensionsPBOPAndOrPostEmploymentBenefitPlansTextBlock id="HHEF00A37BE7FC48E876258E31A5434A4A" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfPensionPostRetirementBenefitOtherThanPensionsPBOPAndOrPostEmploymentBenefitPlansTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfAssetsRetirementObligationsAROTextBlock id="HHFAE64936FDD456A2C12F0FE143F13EFB" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfAssetsRetirementObligationsAROTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfRateTreatmentForUnamortizedLossOnReacquiredDebtAndUnamortizedGainOnReacquiredDebtTextBlock id="HHB4C2F5ED33AF365D6A63772234801965" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfRateTreatmentForUnamortizedLossOnReacquiredDebtAndUnamortizedGainOnReacquiredDebtTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantFinancialChangesTextBlock id="HH768D5D4066691F2DD5E2FA0EE9BB7BA0" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantFinancialChangesTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfUnsettledRateProceedingsTextBlock id="HH4BF49E161908A7C6C0D18BFC6FC7874B" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfUnsettledRateProceedingsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantChangesInAccountingMethodsTextBlock id="HHDA140C5C60AD549A3DC7FFC2AA805B32" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantChangesInAccountingMethodsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSubsequentEventsTextBlock id="HH9725BE0CD181FF6138CC550B2291DD1F" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4"></ferc:DisclosureOfSubsequentEventsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="HHD790CCB2EDB33AB49E3957CEAFD4E7D7" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;n/a&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="HHFB845F6A13C048AB10E053139FD9FDD6" contextRef="HH6F430EA68AFC29D1DD829B0DDD45FFF4">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;NOTE 1 Company Business&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Kinetica Energy Express, LLC, a Texas limited liability company, ("KEE"), (The Company) is a wholly-owned, subsidiary of Kinetica Partners, LLC, a Texas limited liability company. The Company provides natural gas transmission from producing fields located in federal waters of the Gulf of Mexico as well as in adjacent state waters and onshore property.&amp;#160; In addition, the Company provides associated liquids transportation and separation of condensate and dehydration of natural gas.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;NOTE 2 Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is regulated by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC).The financial statements presented herein are prepared in accordance with the accounting regulations of the FERC, as set forth in the applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting separate from accounting principles generally accepted in the United States.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The most significant differences between accounting principles generally accepted in the United States and the financial statements presented herein are the classification of certain accounts on the balance sheet, statement of income, and the statement of cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The preparation of our financial statements requires the use of estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities, revenue, expenses and our disclosures in these financial statements. While the company believes its estimates are appropriate, actual results can, and often do, differ from those estimates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;We recognized revenue as service is provided. Customers are invoiced and the related revenue is recorded in the period transportation services on our pipeline are provided to customers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company considers cash and cash equivalents as cash on hand, cash in bank accounts, and highly liquid investments with original maturities of three months or less.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trade Accounts Receivable&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Trade accounts receivable are recorded at the invoiced amount and do not bear interest, except for those receivables subject to late charges. The Company maintains an allowance for doubtful accounts for estimated losses on accounts receivable due from shippers and operators if it is determined the Company will not collect all or part of the outstanding receivable balance. The Company regularly reviews its allowance for doubtful accounts and establishes or adjusts the allowance as necessary using the specific identification method. Account balances are charged to the allowance after all means of collection have been exhausted and the potential for recovery is no longer considered probable. Accounts charged to the allowance in 2025 and 2024 were $1,754,811 and $0, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Materials and Supplies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company&amp;#8217;s inventory consists of materials and supplies. The materials and supplies are valued at cost with cost determined using the average cost method.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Property, plant and equipment are stated at original cost of construction less accumulated depreciation. The Company capitalizes all construction related direct labor and material costs. The costs of renewals and betterments that extend the useful life or increase the expected output of property, plant and equipment are also capitalized. The costs of repairs, replacements and major maintenance projects that do not extend the useful life or increase the expected output of property, plant and equipment, are expensed as incurred.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Depreciation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company uses the composite (group) method to depreciate regulated property, plant and equipment. Under this method, assets with similar useful lives and other characteristics are grouped and depreciated as one asset. The depreciation rate is applied to the total cost of the group until its net book value equals its salvage value. All asset groups are depreciated using the FERC approved rates of 0.47% for depreciation plus 0.40% for negative salvage. Depreciation expense is determined by multiplying the Group rate by the total cost of the assets in the Group for that period. Once the Group rate and the average service life are determined, they normally are not changed despite the addition and disposition of individual assets. This implicitly assumes that the service lives of new assets approximate those of individual assets they replace.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;When property, plant, and equipment are retired, the Company charges accumulated depreciation and amortization for the original cost of the assets in addition to the cost to remove, sell, or dispose of the assets, less their salvage value. The Company does not recognize a gain or loss unless an entire operating unit is sold or retired. The Company includes gains or losses on dispositions of operating units in income.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Computer Software&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Computer software, which is included within intangibles, a component of utility plant, is stated at cost and generally is amortized using a 0.47% depreciation rate. To the extent that related parties share computer software, the costs of the capitalized software may be allocated among those related parties benefiting.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Natural Gas Imbalances&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Natural gas imbalances occur when the actual amount of natural gas delivered to or received from a pipeline system differs from the amount of natural gas scheduled to be delivered or received. These imbalance can be settled through cash outs (purchases or sales at the current month index price) or in kind (with natural gas at some time in the future) subject to the terms of our tariff or agreement. We value these imbalances due to or from shippers and operators at the current market index price. Imbalances due from others are reported on the balance sheet as miscellaneous current and accrued assets. Imbalances owed to others are reported on the balance sheet as miscellaneous current and accrued liabilities. The Company classifies all imbalances as current as the Company expects to settle them within a year.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company accounts for asset retirement obligations pursuant to the provisions of Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) Accounting Standards Codification (ASC) 410 20, Asset Retirement Obligations. ASC 410-20 requires the Company to record the fair value of an asset retirement obligation as a liability in the period in which it incurs a legal obligation associated with the retirement of tangible long lived assets that result from the acquisition, construction, development, and/or normal use of the assets. ASC 410 20 also requires the Company to record a corresponding asset that is depreciated over the life of the asset. Subsequent to the initial measurement of the asset retirement obligation, the obligation is to be adjusted at the end of each period to reflect the passage of time and changes in the estimated future cash flows underlying the obligation. The Company has determined it has legal obligations associated with its natural gas pipelines and related facilities. The obligations associated with its transmission facilities relate primarily to purging and sealing the pipelines if they are abandoned.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is also required to operate and maintain its natural gas pipeline system, and intends to do so as long as supply and demand for natural gas exist, which the Company expects for the foreseeable future. Therefore, the Company believes its natural gas pipelines have indeterminate lives and, accordingly, has recorded no asset retirement obligation as of December 31, 2025 and 2024, respectively. The Company continues to evaluate its asset retirement obligations and future developments that could impact the amounts it records.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;NOTE 3 Income Taxes&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;The Company is organized as a Texas limited liability company and is disregarded for federal income tax purposes. Income taxes associated with its operations are the responsibility of its members and would not generally be reflected in its financial statements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;NOTE 4 Related Party Transactions&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;Balances in accounts receivable and accounts payables with Associated Companies include activities in the normal course of business. Direct charges, allocated salaries, overhead expenses paid by the parent company, Kinetica Partners, LLC (KPLLC), and cash receipts advanced from KEE are part of account receivables and /or payables to Associated Companies in KEE&amp;#8217;s financial statements.&amp;#160; Occasionally, KEE will have receipts that belong to Kinetica Midstream Energy, LLC (KME) and Kinetica Deepwater Express, LLC (KDE), affiliated companies. In the same manner KME and KDE will have sale receipts that belong to KEE. These transactions create an intercompany receivable / (payable) from / to KEE. At December 31, 2025 and 2024, the company had accounts receivable / (payable) as follows (in 000's):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="font-family: sans-serif; font-size: medium;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
